  "summary": "The News Tribune reports on the growing adoption of Flock Safety automated license plate readers by Pierce County, WA jurisdictions, including Lakewood (31-36 cameras, $224,000), Puyallup ($246,000 two-year contract for ~37 cameras), and Eatonville (considering a five-year extension). The article details how the technology works, ACLU privacy concerns, the lack of Washington state regulation, and criticism that local approvals were placed on routine consent agendas with little public debate.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "There are 40 agencies currently using Flock Safety's technology in Washington, according to the city of Puyallup, which entered into a two-year contract with the company for $246,000 on Dec. 5",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "In Washington state, except for Seattle's Surveillance Ordinance passed in 2017, there are no legal standards governing the use of ALPR systems or the management of the data they generate",
      "paragraph_idx": 29
    },
    {
      "quote": "these items are considered routine operational purchases that do not warrant additional public outreach",
      "paragraph_idx": 36
    },
    {
      "quote": "The town is mulling cutting data retention in half to 15 days after a City Council member requested it do so before entering into the five-year contract",
      "paragraph_idx": 25
    }
  ],
